
  This is the part with the real decisions in it, so I'll be specific.

  [1mTwo delivery surfaces, with a clear rule for each.[0m The in-page banner is the guaranteed floor — it always works, needs
  no permission, and can never be blocked. The OS notification is enhancement on top. The rule that decides which
  fires: [1mOS notifications are for "you might not be looking"; the in-page banner is for "you're here now."[0m That resolves
  the catch-up case cleanly — on startup you're by definition looking at the app, so catch-up renders an in-page
  summary and does [3mnot[0m fire system notifications. Opening a tab and getting twelve OS notifications for tasks that went
  overdue last week is hostile, and the aggregate-notification workaround is just a quieter version of the same mistake.

  [1mPermission.[0m Never prompt on load — it's the pattern users reflexively deny, and Chrome actively penalizes it. Prompt
  on the first time you set a due date, where the request has visible context. Plus an explicit "Enable reminders"
  control in the UI, which matters more than it looks: [1ma denied permission is sticky and silent.[0m The browser won't
  re-prompt, so without a visible state indicator the app just quietly stops notifying and looks broken. That control
  shows current permission state and, when denied, says reminders are in-page only and what to change in browser
  settings.

  [1mFiring rule[0m — a task is notifiable when all of: not completed, [38;5;153mdueAt[39m is set, [38;5;153mdueAt <= now[39m, and [38;5;153mnotifiedAt[39m is null. The
  ticker runs every 30s. One honest caveat: browsers throttle timers in background tabs, typically to once a minute or
  worse, so a reminder can land up to a minute or so late when the tab is hidden. That's inherent to the no-server
  approach, not a bug we'd later fix.

  [1mEdge cases worth deciding now rather than discovering:[0m

  - [1mChanging a due date resets [38;5;153mnotifiedAt[39m to null.[0m It's a new commitment and should be able to notify again.
  - [1mA task created already overdue gets [38;5;153mnotifiedAt[39m stamped at creation.[0m Entering something you forgot last Tuesday
    shouldn't immediately shout at you.
  - [1mCompleting or deleting removes it from consideration[0m — no notification for work already done.
  - [1mSleep and clock changes need no handling.[0m Because the model pulls (compare [38;5;153mdueAt[39m to current time) rather than
    scheduling timers at absolute future moments, waking a laptop after two days just catches up on the next tick. This
    is the main reason I'd avoid [38;5;153msetTimeout[39m-per-task, which breaks on sleep, clock adjustment, and long durations.

  Does the notification behavior look right?

